Gateway backup inventory contract and limitations
================================================

The OPNsense core backup inventory uses GET /api/core/backup/backups/this.
The host selector is intentionally fixed to this firewall; remote OPNcentral
providers are not queried. Its items contain id, time_iso, filesize and
description. FreeSDN normalizes these into filename, timestamp, size_bytes and
description, preserving list order and zero-byte sizes. A successful empty
items list remains a confirmed empty inventory.

The previous client omitted the required host selector and the adapter looked
for a backups field rather than items, so valid history could be lost. The
gateway service also turned adapter failures into HTTP 200 empty inventories.

The service now returns a fixed HTTP 502 on failure or malformed metadata and
HTTP 501 on an explicitly unsupported operation. Admission denials remain
unchanged. Metadata must be a list of distinct safe filenames and correctly
typed fields. Extra provider fields, including configuration bodies, are not
returned. Provider exception text is not included in inventory diagnostics;
shared gateway adapter cleanup also avoids logging raw exception text.

Reference, retrieved 2026-09-25:
https://github.com/opnsense/core/blob/26.7.4/src/opnsense/mvc/app/controllers/OPNsense/Core/Api/BackupController.php
https://docs.opnsense.org/development/api/core/core.html

Qualification includes PostgreSQL-backed HTTP admission/response tests and a
separate real HTTPX/TLS probe against an owned synthetic OPNsense-shaped server.
The service probe explicitly doubles vendor I/O; the TLS probe uses the actual
OPNsense client and adapter, overriding only its fixture destination and trust.
Neither is a real-appliance compatibility, backup creation, restore, or data
durability test. This does not expand the vendor hardware support matrix.

Remaining work includes per-table unavailable/stale states and size_bytes
display in the frontend, validating vendor create/delete/revert contracts, and
FreeSDN-managed off-device artifact retention and recovery. An inventory entry
alone proves neither its contents nor restorability. The current frontend's
existing query error banner can surface these HTTP failures; it is unchanged.
The OpenWRT adapter's existing static empty history response is also unchanged;
it is not evidence that a real appliance has no backup files. Explicitly marking
that unsupported history capability remains a separate correction.
